{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfwPbSFdUbyWjrpKTCVJ2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard to look at the panels, and I haven't seen the rule fire.\n\n**Data:** The data view uses `timestamp` as its time field. The fields I used were `response` (a string, so I used `response.keyword`), `extension.keyword` and `bytes`. It also has client IP, geo, machine OS, URL and referer fields.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-20dcf883 Web logs overview\" has four panels:\n- Total requests (a single number)\n- Requests over time (area chart)\n- Requests by response code (pie chart)\n- Average bytes by extension (bar chart)\n\nIt's at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-20dcf883-web-logs-overview-c0770f6ce429\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-20dcf883 5xx spike\" is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and counts documents whose `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]` over a 5-minute window. It fires when the count is above 10.\n\nThe rule has no action attached, so when it fires it only shows up as an alert in Kibana and doesn't notify anyone. Kibana's sample logs are timestamped relative to when they were installed, so the rule may not see many recent 5xx documents."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":163,"cache_read_input_tokens":20084,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":163},"output_tokens":459,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4eb1cb7-866c-5bbf-8d81-a8d5acd83be3","uuid":"64295a76-4b34-4037-9e56-1487553d4f95","timestamp":"2026-10-12T00:23:50.464Z","request_id":"req_011CfwPbS4imtGqvnJ8ek9Ry"}
